module contract;

// Fluent Bit input-chunk destructor — resource-projection vocabulary version 1.
//
// Card ADDRESS-OF-MEMBER-DESTROY-UNDECLARED. This module declares ONE TRUSTED
// ASSUMPTION about one C function. It verifies nothing: the C body is not
// analyzed by this loader, and a declaration is a premise. Any verdict that
// rests on this module is a verdict RELATIVE TO this declaration and is never
// a proof of the C implementation.
//
// The body was read at Fluent Bit `66910c10a4d7eafa810b84229cf2dcf7b0f26f97`,
// `src/flb_input_chunk.c:2704-2807`, and is cited by line below. The C
// declaration in force is `include/fluent-bit/flb_input_chunk.h:111`:
//
//     int flb_input_chunk_destroy(struct flb_input_chunk *ic, int del);
//
// ## Why this function is declared although the analysed unit defines it
//
// `flb_input_chunk_destroy` is defined in `src/flb_input_chunk.c`, which is
// itself a unit of the pinned corpus, so this is the arrangement cards
// PROD-CONTRACTS and NEIGHBOURS already established for
// `flb_input_chunk_get_name`, `generate_chunk_name` and
// `input_chunk_write_header`: a declared contract ALWAYS wins over a
// W4-inferred summary, a disagreement is reported as a `SummaryConflict`
// diagnostic and never silently resolved
// (`crates/qcore-c-gateway/src/legacy_summary.rs`), and declaring a defined
// function does not stop the unit analysing that function's own body — it only
// fixes what its CALLERS may assume. `flb_input_chunk_destroy`'s own status is
// whatever its body earns, and this module does not move it.
//
// ## The custody statement, read against the body
//
// The body has **exactly one exit**. There is no `return` anywhere between
// `:2704` and the `return 0;` at `:2807`; every conditional in it is a
// statement-level guard, never an early exit. So the following four statements
// run on EVERY path through the function:
//
//     cio_chunk_close(ic->chunk, del);                        // :2796
//     mk_list_del(&ic->_head);                                // :2797
//     if (ic->routes_mask != NULL) { flb_free(ic->routes_mask); ... }  // :2799-2802
//     flb_free(ic);                                           // :2804
//
// Those are exactly the three acquisitions card ABI-TYPEDEF-ADDRESS-OF-MEMBER
// measured `flb_input_chunk_create` handing to its caller through the returned
// `ic` — the `cio_chunk_open` result, the `flb_calloc` result and the
// routes-mask allocation — released in the same order, and nothing the caller
// still owns is left over. The caller's obligation on `ic` is discharged in
// full and unconditionally, which is what `resource_released` says, and — over
// the whole v1 parameter vocabulary — it is the only mode with that
// caller-side effect. `resource_borrowed` would leave a discharged obligation
// standing and `resource_stored` would consume it under another owner that
// does not exist; both would be unfaithful, and an unfaithful declaration is
// worse than `Unknown`.
//
// `nonnull`, and not `nullable`, is the faithful nullability. The FIRST
// statement of the body is
//
//     if (flb_input_chunk_is_up(ic) == FLB_FALSE) { ... }      // :2712
//
// and `flb_input_chunk_is_up` (`:3128`) immediately evaluates `ic->chunk`.
// A null `ic` is undefined behaviour before any guard could see it. Declaring
// `nullable` here would be unfaithful in the PERMISSIVE direction: it would
// discharge a caller's obligation at a site where the projection cannot prove
// the pointer non-null, which is the opposite of fail-closed. `flb_free` is
// declared `nullable` because `free(NULL)` is defined; this is not that.
//
// `del` is an `int` by value. `resource_borrowed` is the only mode the loader
// admits on a scalar, and it is also the true one — the value selects whether
// `cio_chunk_close` unlinks the backing file, and selects nothing about
// custody. This is the same position `chunkio/resource_open_close.qcore` gives
// `cio_chunk_close`'s own `delete`.
//
// The result is `int`, and it is the literal `0` on the one exit. It carries no
// custody, so `resource_return_none()`.
//
// ## NOT CLAIMED, recorded rather than hidden
//
//   * **Nothing about the accounting loop** (`:2718-2740`). It walks
//     `ic->in->config->outputs`, may call `flb_input_chunk_output_size_subtract`
//     and logs; this declaration says nothing about `fs_chunks_size`, about
//     whether the subtraction balances an earlier addition, or about the
//     `ic->fs_counted` flag.
//   * **Nothing about the hash-table removal** (`:2764-2789`). `flb_hash_table_del_ptr`
//     is a "TRY" by upstream's own comment; whether the entry is present, and
//     whether some other table still holds the pointer this call is about to
//     free, is the unmodeled retention this corpus records as B03/R13 and is
//     not decided here. In particular this module does NOT claim the release is
//     safe — it claims it HAPPENS.
//   * **Nothing about `cio_chunk_close` failing.** The body ignores its result,
//     and `flb_free(ic)` runs regardless. The custody statement follows the
//     body, not the wish.
//   * **Nothing about `ic->trace`.** The pinned corpus is compiled with
//     `-DFLB_HAVE_CHUNK_TRACE`, so `flb_chunk_trace_destroy(ic->trace)`
//     (`:2790-2794`) IS in the analysed body. It releases a member, not the
//     caller's `ic`; `flb_chunk_trace_destroy` stays undeclared and nothing
//     here depends on it.
//   * **Nothing about `mk_list_del(&ic->_head)`** beyond its running: whether
//     `ic` was on a list at all is the same unmodeled retention as above.
//   * **`flb_input_chunk_destroy_corrupted` (`:2623`) and
//     `flb_input_chunk_destroy_direct_routes` (`:2483`) are DELIBERATELY LEFT
//     UNDECLARED.** They are different functions with different bodies and
//     different exits, and the second is the callee that
//     `CONSTRUCTOR-RESIDUALS-LEAK-271` rests on. Declaring either on the
//     strength of its neighbour's name would be exactly the
//     unfaithful-declaration defect card P07 recorded, and this card withdraws
//     neither finding. Named `AOM-DESTROY-SIBLINGS-UNDECLARED`.
//
//     **Card AOM-DESTROY-SIBLINGS decided that follow-on, and the warning above
//     was justified by the outcome.** Reading each body on its own terms gave
//     the two functions DIFFERENT answers.
//     `flb_input_chunk_destroy_direct_routes` is now declared in
//     `fluent_bit/input_chunk_destroy_direct_routes.qcore` — with a
//     **`nullable`** release, not this module's `nonnull`, because that body's
//     third statement is `if (!routes) { return; }`. Copying this module's
//     nullability onto it would have been unfaithful.
//     `flb_input_chunk_destroy_corrupted` **remains undeclared**: its reading
//     is written out in that card's report, and its measured payoff is zero,
//     because its single in-corpus call site (`input_chunk_get`, `:2866`;
//     preprocessed L19823) is refused as an indirect memory access before the
//     call rule is reached. Named `AOM-DESTROY-CORRUPTED-UNPAID-PREMISE`.

extern fn flb_input_chunk_destroy(ic: ptr<mut, u8>, del: i32) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_unpublished(ic);
    ensures normal resource_released(ic, nonnull);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(del);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};
